==============================================================================
 XT - an XTree-style file manager for 6502DOS
==============================================================================

 The tree of folders is at the left, and the files of the selected folder at
 the right. Row 0 holds the path and the counts. The bottom two rows hold the
 commands. Hold Ctrl for the Ctrl commands and Alt for the Alt commands. The
 plain commands change with the pane. Prompts and messages use the same rows.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Navigation
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Up, Down    Move one row in the pane
   PgUp, PgDn  Move one page in the pane
   Tab         Move between the panes
   Right       Tree: move to the files, and log a folder that is not logged.
               A folder with no files to show expands.
   Left        Tree: collapse the folder. Files: move to the tree.
   F1          Show this help
   Q           Quit in the folder XT started in, after a prompt
   Alt-Q       Quit in the selected folder, after a prompt

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Tree pane
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Enter       Log and expand the folder, or collapse it
   +           Log and expand the folder
   -           Collapse the folder
   M           Make a folder in the selected folder
   R           Rename the folder. The drive root is refused.
   D           Delete the folder, after a prompt. It must be logged and empty.
               The drive root is refused.
   S, G        Show the files of every logged folder
   B           Show the files of the logged folders of the branch

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 File pane
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   T           Flip the file's tag, and move down
   U           Clear the file's tag, and move down
   V           Run VIEW on the file
   E           Run EDIT on the file
   C           Copy the file to a folder
   M           Move the file to a folder
   R           Rename the file. * and ? in the new name take from the old one.
   D           Delete the file, after a prompt
   Enter, Esc  Move to the tree
   Alt-X       Ask for parameters, and run the .COM file from its folder.
               A key returns to XT.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Either pane
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A           Show the DOS version, the free RAM banks and the free KB of C:
   F           Set the filespec, which selects the files shown. An empty
               answer is *.*

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Showall and Branch: S, G and B
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 The file window fills the screen with the files of many folders. All the file
 commands work, and each acts on the file in its own folder. Ctrl-C and Ctrl-O
 gather tagged files from many folders into one.

 The path in row 0 follows the highlighted file. Only logged folders show, so
 logging more folders widens the list. The filespec still applies.

 The list holds 2048 files. The count in row 0 includes the ones past that.

 Tab, Enter, Left or Esc returns to the two panes.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Ctrl commands
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Ctrl-T      Tag the files that match the filespec
   Ctrl-U      Untag them
   Ctrl-N      Flip their tags
   Ctrl-W      Ask for a wildcard, and tag those of them that match it
   Ctrl-C      Copy the file window's tagged files to a folder
   Ctrl-O      Move them to a folder
   Ctrl-D, Ctrl-X
               Delete them. A prompt asks whether to confirm each. In each
               prompt, A deletes the rest and Esc stops.
   Ctrl-V      Run VIEW on them in turn. Q or Esc in VIEW goes to the next.
   Ctrl-S, Ctrl-E
               Ask for a text, and untag those that do not hold it, in either
               case. Esc stops, and the files not reached stay tagged.
   Ctrl-F, Ctrl-G
               Ask for a filespec, log the folders of the disk that hold a
               match, and list the matches across the screen. Esc stops the
               walk. In the list, Enter goes to the highlighted file and keeps
               that filespec. Leaving another way brings the old one back.

 A working loop: tag the files of interest, narrow them with Ctrl-S, then read
 what is left with Ctrl-V.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Alt commands
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Alt-J       Ask for a path from C:\, and log and expand each folder down to
               it. This reaches a folder that was never logged.
   Alt-R       Forget the folder's files and subfolders until it is logged
               again
   Alt-T       Tag the files that match the filespec in the logged folders of
               the branch
   Alt-U       Untag every file of every logged folder, whatever the filespec
   Alt-S       Pick the next sort: name, extension, size, date. It runs when
               Alt is released.
   Alt-F3      Read the disk again: new subfolders in the tree, the files in
               the file window. The files' tags clear.
   Alt-P       Delete the highlighted folder and all under it, after the word
               prune is typed in lower case. It reads the disk, so a folder
               that is not logged prunes too. The drive root and a branch that
               holds the current directory are refused.
   Alt-F10     Settings, below

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Copy and move
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 The destination is a path from C:\. A folder that does not exist is made,
 after a prompt. A name that exists asks Yes, No, All or Skip all. Move takes
 files only. A copy takes today's date.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 In a prompt for text
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Up          List the prompt's last 8 answers. Up and Down move, Enter takes
               one and Esc goes back. An answer over 64 characters is not kept.
   F2          Copy and move: point at the folder in the tree. Up, Down, PgUp
               and PgDn move, + or Right opens a folder, - or Left collapses
               it, Enter picks it, and Esc or Q goes back.
   Enter       Accept
   Esc         Cancel

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 In VIEW: V, Ctrl-V and this help
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Up, Down    Move one line
   PgUp, PgDn  Move one page. Space moves a page down.
   Home, End   Go to the first line, or the last page
   Left, Right Move the view 8 columns sideways
   W           Turn wrap on and off
   F, /        Ask for a text, and find it from the top line down, in any case
   N           Find it again
   Q, Esc      End

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Settings: Alt-F10
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   T           Pick the next theme of C:\sys\themes, which shows at once
   S           Pick the next sort, which runs at the end
   Enter       Save both in the section [XT] of C:\6502DOS.INI
   Esc         Bring back the old ones

 With no theme in [XT], the registry key THEME names it.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Limits and files
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Folders     The tree holds 254. Find stops at that, with a message.
   Files       A list holds 2048. The file records fill the free RAM banks.
   History     C:\sys\xt holds a .HIS file for each prompt.
   This help   XT.HLP, beside XT.COM
   HELP XT     A short list of the keys, for the shell

